{
  "id": "modal-verbs",
  "level": "A1",
  "title": "Modalverben und Imperative",
  "group": "Verbs",
  "sourceFile": "modalverben.html",
  "overviewSource": "modalverbenexpl.html",
  "overview": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "Modal verbs express ability, permission, necessity, intention, advice, or preference. The conjugated modal is in position 2 and the main verb goes to the end as an infinitive."
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "content": "können — can, be able to\nmüssen — must, have to\nwollen — want to\ndürfen — may, be allowed to\nsollen — should, be supposed to\nmögen — like\nmöchten — would like"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "content": "Present tense conjugation"
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "content": "",
        "rows": [
          [
            "",
            "können",
            "müssen",
            "wollen",
            "dürfen",
            "sollen",
            "mögen",
            "möchten"
          ],
          [
            "ich",
            "kann",
            "muss",
            "will",
            "darf",
            "soll",
            "mag",
            "möchte"
          ],
          [
            "du",
            "kannst",
            "musst",
            "willst",
            "darfst",
            "sollst",
            "magst",
            "möchtest"
          ],
          [
            "er/sie/es",
            "kann",
            "muss",
            "will",
            "darf",
            "soll",
            "mag",
            "möchte"
          ],
          [
            "wir",
            "können",
            "müssen",
            "wollen",
            "dürfen",
            "sollen",
            "mögen",
            "möchten"
          ],
          [
            "ihr",
            "könnt",
            "müsst",
            "wollt",
            "dürft",
            "sollt",
            "mögt",
            "möchtet"
          ],
          [
            "sie/Sie",
            "können",
            "müssen",
            "wollen",
            "dürfen",
            "sollen",
            "mögen",
            "möchten"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "The singular forms often change their stem. Ich and er/sie/es have no personal ending and are identical: ich kann, er kann."
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "content": "",
        "de": "Ich kann Deutsch sprechen. Du musst heute lernen.",
        "note": "conjugated modal in position 2 · infinitive at the end"
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "content": "Imperative — commands"
      },
      {
        "type": "table",
        "content": "",
        "rows": [
          [
            "Addressing",
            "Formation",
            "kommen",
            "lesen"
          ],
          [
            "du",
            "verb stem; omit du",
            "Komm!",
            "Lies!"
          ],
          [
            "ihr",
            "present ihr-form; omit ihr",
            "Kommt!",
            "Lest!"
          ],
          [
            "Sie",
            "infinitive + Sie",
            "Kommen Sie!",
            "Lesen Sie!"
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": "Modal verbs themselves rarely form ordinary imperatives. Use the imperative of the action verb (Warte!) or a statement with müssen/sollen (Du musst warten. Ihr sollt leise sein.). Sein is irregular: sei, seid, seien Sie."
      },
      {
        "type": "example",
        "content": "",
        "de": "Sei leise! / Seid leise! / Seien Sie leise!",
        "note": "imperative of sein for du / ihr / Sie"
      }
    ]
  },
